WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.035 --> 00:00:01.410
STEPHANIE WONG:
Hi, I'm Stephanie

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.868
and today I'll show
you how you can

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:05.640
run your functions in any
language as serverless code

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.050
on Google Cloud.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.860
No matter if you're a Ruby,
.NET, Java, PHP, Python,

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.020
or Go developer, fear no more.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.170
You can now seamlessly deploy
your function to Cloud Run.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.910
In the last year,
the Serverless team

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.590
has been working to
support developers,

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.730
no matter which
language they use.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.270
In fact, we have seven
plug-and-play libraries that

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.610
allow you to run your
code, in any language,

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.440
functions-as-a-service stat.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.530
Last year, we launched Cloud
Run, a fully managed serverless

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.570
compute platform
based on Docker.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.150
It automatically scales and
load balances your stateless

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:39.630
containers while language client
libraries automatically work.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.180
You pay per request, so you
can do what matters most--

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.310
build great applications.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.840
We've now open-sourced
a framework

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.670
that powers Google
Cloud functions,

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.860
called the Functions Framework.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.630
It's a set of
open-source libraries

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.420
that minimizes the amount of
boilerplate and configuration

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.420
needed from you to create a
runable, stateless container.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.700
In fact, this framework
is a dependency

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.390
that's used in Cloud functions.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.420
So you'll get the
exact same core runtime

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.340
environment and behavior.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.710
It handles-- setting
up a web server,

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.900
adding routing to the
server, and invoking

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.220
function code in response to an
HTTP request or a CloudEvent.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.080
What this means
is you can simply

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.760
install the Functions
Framework library

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.820
for the language of your
choice and write a function.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.820
You don't need to emulate your
function when testing locally.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.670
Let's dive into each language.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:29.580
The Node Functions Framework
uses Express.js under the hood,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.030
adopts Node's event-based
model that's asynchronous,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.310
has non-blocking
I/O, and supports

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.760
promises for handling requests,
and is an excellent choice

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.310
for scaling apps both
horizontally and vertically.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.480
The Go Functions Framework
uses the native HTTP Package

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.850
for handling requests
and responses,

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.010
provides excellent
cross-platform support

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.590
and static type-checking
for your function.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.510
And it's a great option for raw
performance and computation.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.660
The Java Functions Framework
uses Java and Maven

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.300
for building and deploying
functions from source.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.040
It's not limited
to Java, meaning

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.160
you can use different JVM
languages, such as Kotlin,

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.960
Groovy, and Scala.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.230
You can use a Maven plugin and
run mvn function:run to run

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.037
locally and mvn
function:deploy to deploy.

00:02:16.037 --> 00:02:18.120
And it's supported by
frameworks like Spring Cloud

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.280
Function and Micronaut.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.650
For Python users, the
Function Framework

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.250
uses Flask, a popular Python
web framework, under the hood.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.980
By default, it uses
Gunicorn as an HTTP server,

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.980
but also provides
a WSGI app so you

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.100
can use any HTTP server
that supports WSGI.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.100
And it's installable
from PyPI with "pip

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.020
install functions-framework."

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.840
The Ruby Functions Framework
uses a standard Ruby Rack

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.020
interface for HTTP
requests and CloudEvents.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.990
You can install your own bundle
and access all the libraries

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.600
and capabilities of
the Ruby ecosystem.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.720
And it has built-in helpers
for building test fixtures

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.870
and invoking functions
in a test environment.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.810
The .NET Functions Framework
is built on ASP.NET Core

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.440
in an idiomatic way,
incorporating familiar

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.720
dependency injection.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.580
The templates are distributed
as NuGet packages.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.370
Just use dotnet
new to get started.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.470
And it includes a
dedicated testing package

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:17.040
so you can write unit and
integration tests easily.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.430
The PHP Functions Framework
is usable with PHP's built-in

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.940
server, supports
symphony/http-foundation,

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.130
and will soon support PSR-7.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.960
You can install
your own packages

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.230
and access all the libraries
and capabilities of the PHP

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.430
ecosystem.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.040
Keep in mind, you are not
limited to these languages.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.380
Any language that can be
run in a Docker container

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.892
can be deployed to Cloud Run.

00:03:38.892 --> 00:03:41.100
Once you've built your
application with the Functions

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.290
Framework, Cloud Run
lets you get your code

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.010
into production in seconds.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.610
Just run a gcloud command
to deploy your stateless

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.190
container, and Cloud
Run will do the rest.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:53.250
Listen to requests and events,
pass them to your code,

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.630
and scale them up and
down to meet user demand.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.060
With the Functions
Framework, you

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.400
can run your applications
as serverless code

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.790
in any language, port workloads
across any serverless platform

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.620
with an open-source
framework in Docker,

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.280
and spin up a local development
server for quick testing.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.790
Check out cloud.google.com/run
to learn more.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

